{"id":"q25","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"linda","question":"A health survey samples adult men at random. Is it at least as likely that a sampled man has had one or more heart attacks as that he is over 55 years old and has had one or more heart attacks?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"Every man who is over 55 and has had a heart attack has necessarily had a heart attack.","confidence":0.98,"lean":1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The event 'over 55 and heart attack' is a subset of the event 'heart attack'.","confidence":0.98,"lean":1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":0.0},{"text":"A subset is never more probable than the set containing it, so P(heart attack) ≥ P(over 55 and heart attack).","confidence":0.98,"lean":1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Adding a condition to an event can only keep its probability the same or lower it (monotonicity).","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Because sampling is random, the probabilities equal the population proportions and the subset relation holds exactly.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The over-55 heart-attack group might seem comparably or more likely because older men are at risk.","confidence":0.15,"lean":-1,"theta":0.2356,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Judging the more detailed description as more probable is the conjunction fallacy.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Comparing conditional probabilities such as P(heart attack | over 55) with P(heart attack) could give a different answer.","confidence":0.2,"lean":-1,"theta":0.3142,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The question asks about the joint event, so the conditional-probability reading does not apply.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The subset relation, following from the axioms of probability, decides the answer over intuition.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0